# Ocean Currents and Tides: Rivers in the Sea

> Surface waters stream in vast loops and the sea itself rises and
> falls twice a day. This lesson charts the major currents, their
> climate effects, and the astronomy that drives tides.

## 1. What Drives the Currents

Currents are set moving by a committee of causes: solar heating (which
expands and piles up equatorial water), **prevailing winds** (the chief
driver of surface drift), gravity (pulling the pile back down),
**Coriolis deflection** (curving the flow clockwise in the Northern
Hemisphere, anticlockwise in the Southern), and density differences of
temperature and salinity that power the slow deep circulation.

## 2. The Major Currents

@diagram tree id="world-ocean-currents" layout="tree" aria="Major warm and cold ocean currents"
  Currents -> "Warm currents"
  Currents -> "Cold currents"
  "Warm currents" -> "Gulf Stream, North Atlantic Drift"
  "Warm currents" -> "Kuroshio, Agulhas"
  "Cold currents" -> "Oyashio, Labrador"
  "Cold currents" -> "Canary, Benguela, Humboldt"
@/diagram

| Ocean | Warm currents | Cold currents |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Atlantic | Gulf Stream, North Atlantic Drift | Canary, Labrador, Benguela |
| Pacific | Kuroshio | Oyashio, Californian, Humboldt (Peru) |
| Indian | Agulhas (Mozambique Channel) | West Australian |

The **North Atlantic Drift**, the Gulf Stream's extension, keeps the
Norwegian coast and north-west Europe ice-free in winter — Palm trees
grow in western Scotland at nearly 57°N. Cold currents do the opposite:
the **Benguela** and **Humboldt** chill the air above them and help
strand the Namib and Atacama deserts beside the sea.

[example]
Where warm and cold waters meet, nutrients well up and plankton bloom:
the Grand Banks off Newfoundland (Gulf Stream against Labrador) and
the seas off Japan (Kuroshio against Oyashio) are the classic fishing
grounds.
[/example]

## 3. El Niño and La Niña

Every few years the trade winds over the Pacific slacken; warm water
that normally piles up in the west sloshes east. This is **El Niño**.
The Peru (Humboldt) current weakens, upwelling stops, and the great
anchoveta fishery collapses, while droughts strike Australia and parts
of Asia and floods soak Peru. The see-saw of pressure between the two
sides of the Pacific is the **El Niño–Southern Oscillation (ENSO)**.
**La Niña** is the strengthening opposite — cooler eastern Pacific
water, stronger trades, and often a brisker Indian monsoon.

## 4. Tides

[definition]
**Tides** are the periodic rise and fall of sea level, produced mainly
by the gravitational pull of the **Moon** (the Sun contributes about
46% of the Moon's force). One high tide follows another roughly every
12 hours and 25 minutes on most coasts.
[/definition]

| Tide | Happens when | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Spring tide | New and full moon — Sun, Moon, and Earth aligned | Highest highs, lowest lows |
| Neap tide | Quarter moons — Sun and Moon at right angles (quadrature) | Weakest tides |
| Perigean spring | New or full moon near perigee (Moon closest) | Exceptionally large range |

| Pattern | Rhythm | Example coast |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Semi-diurnal | Two highs, two lows a day (most common) | Most of the Indian coast |
| Diurnal | One high, one low a day | Parts of the Gulf of Tonkin |
| Mixed | Two uneven highs and lows | Parts of the US west coast |

## 5. Why Tides Matter

- **Navigation** — deep-draught ships enter harbours riding the tide.
- **Fishing** — currents stirred by tides concentrate fish.
- **Cleaning** — tides flush estuaries of silt and waste.
- **Power** — the fast, high tides of India's **Gulf of Kutch** and
  **Gulf of Khambhat** offer the country's best tidal-energy
  prospects.

[warning]
Tidal bores and storm surges arriving at high tide can multiply
coastal flooding — a reminder that the sea keeps a strict timetable
and does not consult us about it.
[/warning]

## 6. Quick Revision

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| Chief driver of surface currents? | Prevailing winds |
| Current that warms NW Europe? | North Atlantic Drift |
| Cold current off Peru? | Humboldt (Peru) current |
| El Niño first hits? | The Peru fishery |
| Dominant tide-maker? | The Moon (Sun adds ~46%) |
| Spring tides occur at? | New and full moon |
| India's tidal-energy gulfs? | Kutch and Khambhat |
